  "headline": "Growth is winning the tape and my fiscal-dominance thesis is losing the month",
  "body": [
    "Today was a growth day, not a financing squeeze. Yields rose about 5 basis points across the curve while the S&P 500 added 0.3% to sit at 95% of its 52-week range, the dollar slipped 0.3% off the top of its own range, and copper firmed. Rising stocks alongside rising yields is the boom signature, and I have to say it plainly: the fiscal-dominance read I carry is on the wrong side of this month, and the ground it is losing is gold.",
    "Yesterday I called this a dollar-and-energy squeeze with no fear in it. The no-fear half held, with the VIX at 16.9 and in the bottom fifth of its range. The squeeze half loosened: the dollar gave back and bonds sold rather than rallying on crude as a tax on demand, so my benign bond read is on probation rather than confirmed.",
    "The tell I trust most is gold's refusal to bid when the dollar softens. The dollar fell 0.3% and gold did nothing, parked at 8% of its range and down 6% year to date, which says nobody is reaching for a hedge against the dollar's purchasing power. The one fingerprint in my favor is that the 30-year rose 5.6 basis points against 3 for the 5-year, a small bear-steepening, but that is one day set against a month in which the 30-10 spread flattened half a basis point. I take gold and stocks over one session's curve: the market is pricing growth, not financing stress, and MOVE at 94.5, two-thirds of the way up its range, says bonds are watchful rather than breaking.",
    "Drop Site News reports no clear winner in the Israeli elections and a US boat strike that killed five, alongside its earlier report that the UAE is covertly helping Ansarallah against Saudi-backed forces in Yemen. Brent was flat at $110 with realized volatility at roughly half its own year's median, so the market has built a supply premium and added nothing for either story today. The bill is still landing where the thesis says it should: USD/INR is up 0.5% on the week and sits at 81% of its range, while the yen at 158 is mid-range and unstressed.",
    "My book: own copper and Brent, keep gold as insurance rather than a trade, stay away from the long bond at 5.36% with oil at $110 even though the month says duration has paid, and prefer the Canadian dollar to the rupee. I am wrong if gold breaks its 52-week low near $3,960 while the dollar keeps falling; that would mean the hedge has no buyer and the thesis is broken, not early. What brings it back is the 30-5 spread pushing through 50 basis points with stocks falling, and that is what I am watching."
  ],
